  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nOur weather over the next 12-15 hours will be highly dependent on \nthe cold front currently set up over western Corson and Dewey \nCounties. It will continue to slide east across the forecast area, \ndraped south from the main surface low pushing across south central \nCanada. Expect the cold front to cross central South Dakota during \nthe late afternoon and evening hours, and over far northeastern \nSouth Dakota into western Minnesota overnight into early Tuesday \nmorning. Winds will turn out of a west to northwesterly direction \nbehind the front, with the most dramatic/quick change in wind \ndirection this afternoon into the early evening hours across north \ncentral SD. While most locations will remain dry, there is a 30-60% \nchance of showers or thunderstorms with the passage of the cold \nfront. A few stronger storms mainly south will be capable of \nproducing 2 inch diameter hail or wind gusts of 60 to 75 mph. The \nhighest threat area for these stronger storms are over south central \nSouth Dakota south of Pierre, and north of a line from Aberdeen to \nSisseton.\n\nDry high pressure will then build in from the western Canada through \nthe day Thursday. The result will be much drier and windier \nconditions Tuesday afternoon. Relative humidity values will fall into \nthe 25-40% percent range Tuesday, lowest over central South Dakota. \nWinds out of the northwest will increase, and gust 25 to 35 mph. The \nstrongest winds will be over northern SD, with the lightest winds \nsouth of Pierre. Grassland Fire Danger Index values will jump back \ninto the high category for much of central SD Tuesday afternoon. \n\nHighs will remain mainly in the 80s through the period, with lows in \nthe 50s to low 60s. Our next chance of precipitation returns \nThursday night, with the highest chance (20-50%) Friday night into \nSaturday. This aligns with our next low chance of strong to \npotentially severe storms.",
